{"name":"Pony Express","hex":"#726a60","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Pony Express","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A 19th-century mail service delivering messages across North America by means of riders on horseback."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Pony_Express"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pony Express","blocks":[{"text":"The Pony Express was an American express mail service that used relays of horse-mounted riders between Missouri and California. It was operated by the Central Overland California and Pikes Peak Express Company."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Pony_Express%2760_West_bound_1860.jpg/330px-Pony_Express%2760_West_bound_1860.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pony_Express","wikidata":"Q859130"},{"title":"Pony Express (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Pony Express is a 1953 American Western film directed by Jerry Hopper, filmed in Kanab, Utah, and starring Charlton Heston as Buffalo Bill, Forrest Tucker as Wild Bill Hickok, Jan Sterling as a Calamity Jane-type character, and Rhonda Fleming. The story is largely based on the 1925 silent film The Pony Express while the threat of a Californian secession is taken from Frontier Pony Express (1939)."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/6c/Pony_Express_FilmPoster.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pony_Express_(film)","wikidata":"Q3283680"},{"title":"Pony","blocks":[{"text":"A pony is a type of small horse, usually under a specific height at full maturity. Ponies often have thicker coats, manes and tails, compared to larger horses, and proportionally shorter legs, wider barrels, heavier bone, thicker necks and shorter heads. In modern use, breed registries and horse shows may define a pony as measuring at the withers below a certain height; height limits varying from about 14 hands to 14.3 hands. Some distinguish between horse or pony based on its breed or phenotype, regardless of its height. The word pony derives from the old French poulenet, a diminutive of poulain meaning foal, a young, immature horse. A full-sized horse may sometimes be called a pony as a term of endearment."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Gracie-rhs2005.jpg/330px-Gracie-rhs2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pony","wikidata":"Q188828"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Pony_Express_Poster.jpg/500px-Pony_Express_Poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Pony_Express_Poster.jpg/500px-Pony_Express_Poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pony Express advertisement","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APony_Express_Poster.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Alexander_Majors_Pony_Express.jpg/330px-Alexander_Majors_Pony_Express.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Alexander_Majors_Pony_Express.jpg/330px-Alexander_Majors_Pony_Express.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Alexander Majors","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlexander_Majors_Pony_Express.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Pony-express-joseph.jpg/500px-Pony-express-joseph.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Pony-express-joseph.jpg/1280px-Pony-express-joseph.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"